{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And you shall be a blessing</b> — You shall be the blessing with which they bless themselves, saying, 'May Elohim make you like Abraham.' And He further added that all the families of the earth shall bless themselves by him — not the people of his land alone. Or 'ונברכו בך' — that they shall be blessed on his account.\n\nNow, this interpretation does not explain the entire matter. For what reason would HaShem say to him, 'Leave your land and I will do you good' — a goodness unlike any in the world — without first stating that Abraham was a servant of Elohim or a perfect tzaddik, or without stating a reason for leaving the land, namely that his going to another land would bring nearness to Elohim? The way of Scripture is to say, 'Walk before Me and obey My voice and I will do you good,' as with David and Solomon, and as the way of the entire Torah: 'If you walk in My statutes' (Leviticus 26:3), 'If you hearken to hearken to the voice of YHWH your Elohim' (Deuteronomy 28:1). And with Isaac He said, 'For the sake of Abraham My servant' (Genesis 26:24). But to promise him on account of merely leaving the land has no reason.\n\nRather, the reason is that the people of Ur Kasdim did great evils to him on account of his faith in HaShem, and he fled from them to go to the land of Canaan and was delayed in Haran. So He said to him to leave these as well and to do as he had originally intended — that his divine service would be his, and that he would call the children of men in the name of YHWH in the chosen land, and there He would make His name great, and the nations would bless themselves by him — not as they did to him in Ur Kasdim, where they would shame him and curse him and cast him into the pit or the fiery furnace. And He said to him that He would bless those who bless him, and if a single individual curses him, he would be cursed.\n\nThis is the reason for the interpretation, but the Torah did not wish to elaborate on the beliefs of idol worshippers or to explain the affair that took place between him and the Kasdim regarding the faith, just as it abbreviated concerning the generation of Enosh and their reasoning regarding the idolatry that they invented."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {0 "The Hebrew abbreviation הקב\"ה (HaKadosh Baruch Hu) is rendered as HaShem throughout, per voice rules mapping reverent abbreviations to HaShem."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:27:34.653523200Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}